PR #13 — PhotoSpec schema, Zod validation, registry (US, UK, Schengen)
Branch: feat/photospec-registry        Depends on: #12                Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  The country requirement model. This registry is the moat; the schema decides
  how painful the next 37 countries will be.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] PhotoSpec Zod schema with required provenance, plus a `verification`
      field — see below, it is the most important thing in this task
  [x] Registry keyed by country:document, parsed and frozen at module load
  [x] Schengen base spec authored as a RATIO, because that is the unit the
      standard publishes, plus a deep-merge for per-state overrides
  [x] Validation at registry construction: a malformed or duplicate spec throws
      before anything can serve it. `npm run validate:specs` in CI.
  [x] Staleness helper with an injected clock, so no test can go flaky by
      crossing the boundary on a particular day
  [x] findSpecIn takes its registry, keeping the lookup a pure function

  NEW CONCEPT — `verification: 'verified' | 'provisional'`
  A spec nobody has checked against the issuing authority's own page is
  PROVISIONAL and is not served. All four seeded specs are provisional: the
  values were authored from known figures, but the build environment cannot
  reach travel.state.gov or gov.uk, so nothing has been confirmed at source.
  Presenting an unverified government requirement as authoritative is the exact
  failure this product exists to prevent, so it is a state in the type system
  rather than a caveat in a comment.
  CONSEQUENCE: no country page rendered until a human verified a spec and
  flipped the flag. That was intentional, and it held for four plan items.
  Cleared on 2026-08-27 — see below.

  ── RESOLVED, 2026-08-27 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  All four specifications are VERIFIED. The architect supplied the published
  values from the authorities' own documents after a research pass from here
  reached only search summaries — the egress proxy blocks travel.state.gov,
  www.gov.uk, assets.publishing.service.gov.uk and home-affairs.ec.europa.eu,
  so a primary-source read was not possible from this environment and the flag
  was never going to be flippable by the build.

  FOUR THINGS WERE WRONG, and each had a consequence:

  1. US passport digital.maxBytes was 240_000 — the DS-160 CONSULAR ceiling,
     inherited by a spread. A passport upload accepts 10MB, so every passport
     photograph was being compressed against a budget forty times too small.
     The visa keeps 240KB, and a test now guards that the two are different in
     the direction the mistake actually went.

  2. US head height was authored in millimetres, 25.4-34.9. State publishes the
     same rule three ways — "1 - 1 3/8 inches", "(25 - 35 mm)", and 50%-69% of
     the image height — and they disagree, because an inch is 25.4mm. Enforcing
     the millimetre form rejects a photograph at 25.1mm that the inch form
     accepts. Now authored as the proportion, which is what the biometric
     pipeline operates on.

  3. The Schengen citation pointed at a page containing neither the size nor
     the ratio. Regulation 810/2009 states no dimensions at all; it defers to
     ICAO 9303. A "verified on" line linking to a page without the numbers is
     worse than no citation because it looks checked. Now points at the
     Commission's own ICAO photograph guidelines, which state the 70-80%.

  4. The UK background could not be expressed. gov.uk accepts "plain cream or
     light grey" — one warm, one neutral — and the schema held a single hex
     range. A range wide enough for both is a box in RGB, and a box holds its
     corners: a mint wall would have passed. `hexRange` is now `hexRanges`, a
     list, and a test asserts the colour between the two published ones fails.

  ONE THING WAS RIGHT AND NEARLY BROKEN: the UK permits glasses with
  conditions. A widely-repeated third-party claim says they were banned in
  2018. gov.uk's own wording permits them, and the research pass came close to
  "correcting" a correct value on a competitor's say-so.

  NEW FIELD: `crownDefinition`, the one owed since #18. The US measures to the
  top of the HAIR; the UK and Schengen measure to the crown, meaning the SKULL.
  Several millimetres apart on anyone with volume, which is most of the
  tolerance on a head-height rule. It travels with the specification, and both
  observeFrame and CameraCapture now read it from there rather than taking it
  as a parameter a caller could get wrong.

  STILL OWED: digital.minBytes. Both the US (54KB) and the UK (50KB) publish a
  floor, and nothing reads it, so it is not in the schema — a field with no
  consumer is data that drifts unnoticed. It belongs with a rule that warns
  when the encoder has compressed below the authority's minimum.

  CONSEQUENCE, REVERSED: listServableSpecs() now returns four. Country pages
  can generate.
  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

EDGE CASES
  [x] Inverted bands rejected: head height, eye line and digital edge sizes
  [x] Head height normalised once at the registry boundary and returned in BOTH
      units, with the authored unit preserved so a citation can quote what the
      authority actually published rather than our conversion of it
  [x] Deep merge for Schengen overrides. A shallow spread would replace the
      whole print or background object when a state overrides one field of it,
      silently dropping every sibling key — the spec would still parse, it
      would just be wrong.
  [x] Typed not-found that distinguishes an unknown country from an unsupported
      document, so the page can say the right thing
  [x] Deep-frozen at registry construction
  [x] Two documents in one country (us:passport and us:visa both seeded)
  [x] alternativePrintSizes for authorities accepting more than one
  [x] Source URL required by the schema; a spec without one fails to parse
  [x] Duplicate country:document pairs throw rather than silently overwriting

SEO
  [ ] lastVerified feeds sitemap lastmod and the visible "verified on" line

TESTS
  Unit: schema validation, merge logic, staleness, every seeded spec parses.
  Fixture: a deliberately invalid spec proves the build gate works.

