PR #26 — Upload zone and file input
Branch: feat/upload-zone               Depends on: #25               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  The first thing anybody touches. Everything downstream of this is worthless
  to a reader who could not get their photograph in, and the ways people fail
  to get a photograph in are specific and knowable — so each one is handled by
  name rather than by a single "please try another file".

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Drag-and-drop, click-to-browse, paste-from-clipboard
  [x] Mobile capture attribute for direct camera use — on a SECOND input, not
      on the picker. `capture` on the main picker is the common shortcut and it
      is destructive: on a phone it removes the option to choose an existing
      photograph entirely, so somebody who already has a good one is made to
      take a worse one on the spot. Two inputs, two buttons, both available.
  [x] Model warm-up on pointerenter, once per mount and guarded by a ref
  [x] Inline validation errors with recovery guidance

  [~] MIGRATION: ingestion refusal copy moved into the content module. It used
      to be English sentences built inside src/ingestion. A failure now carries
      a messageId and numbers — a size, a format, the dimensions that fell
      short — and the words are resolved from content at render. This is the
      first screen a reader meets, before anything has worked, so it is the
      worst possible place to have untranslatable text; a second language is
      now a content file rather than a second copy of the ingestion module.
      One code can carry two explanations, which is why messageId is a
      separate field: a HEIC this browser cannot open and a TIFF nobody can
      open are both 'format-not-supported', and one of them is three taps in
      Photos away from working.

  [~] ADDED: a `failure` prop, so the CALLER can hand the zone a refusal to
      render. Half the refusals in the pipeline are only knowable after a
      decoder has run — damaged, too small, animated — and decoding happens
      downstream of this component. Without it those failures had nowhere to
      go but a second error block in a second style, next to the one the
      reader has already learnt to read. A refusal detected here outranks one
      passed in, because that one is about the file just chosen.

  [~] FIXED, found by the new guard below: the drag highlight named three
      design tokens that do not exist (--tk-primary, --tk-surface-raised,
      --tk-focus-ring) and two components shipped in #22 and #24 named two
      more (--tk-skeleton, --tk-surface-sunken). Every one had a fallback, so
      every one rendered something plausible instead of nothing — which is
      exactly why they got through review.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Multiple files dropped — the first is used and the count is shown.
      Refusing a four-photograph drop only makes the reader drop one again.
      Reachable by drop and paste only: the picker is deliberately not
      `multiple`, asserted in the browser suite.
  [x] Folder dropped — recognised by the only signal available from this side
      of the API: no type and no size. A genuinely empty file looks identical
      and that ambiguity cannot be resolved here, so the tie is broken toward
      "not a file" — the folder advice is useful to somebody with an empty
      file, and the empty-file advice is baffling to somebody who dropped a
      folder. A zero-byte file that DOES declare a type keeps its own precise
      refusal.
  [x] Non-image dropped — sniffed from the leading bytes, never from the
      declared MIME type or the filename. Both lie routinely: a .jpg that is
      really a HEIC is the most common upload on iOS, because renaming is what
      people try when a file "will not work".
  [x] dragleave firing on child elements (the classic flicker bug) — the drag
      state is a DEPTH COUNTER, not a boolean. Covered twice: in jsdom, and in
      a real browser where the events are the browser's own. The counter is
      also floored at zero, because browsers do emit unpaired dragleaves and a
      negative count would mean the zone never lit up again.
  [x] Keyboard and screen-reader operable without a mouse — the file inputs
      are visually hidden by clip-path and remain focusable. Not `hidden` and
      not display:none, the usual shortcut, which takes them out of the tab
      order and leaves the styled labels beside them unreachable. The focus
      ring is drawn on the label, since the thing focused is invisible.
      Asserted in a real browser by tabbing to it. Refusals are announced in
      an aria-live="polite" region: assertive would cut across whatever the
      reader is being told.
  [x] Drop outside the zone must not navigate the browser away — a window
      listener swallows dragover and drop. The browser's default for a dropped
      file is to open it, discarding the page and everything done so far. The
      same listener is what makes the zone droppable at all, so there is
      deliberately no second preventDefault on the zone itself.
  [x] Re-uploading while an analysis is in flight — `busy` says so and the
      zone keeps accepting. Refusing the new file would leave the reader
      waiting on an analysis of a photograph they have already replaced.
  [~] iOS Safari camera capture quirks — the separate input and `capture="user"`
      as a hint rather than an instruction (iOS offers the choice anyway, which
      costs a tap; forcing the rear camera costs the photograph). HEIC stays in
      the accept list even though many browsers cannot decode it: leaving it
      out does not stop somebody choosing one, it stops the picker showing them
      their own photographs. NOT verified on a physical iPhone — no device in
      CI. Flagged for the device pass in #34.

  [x] Reads only the first 32 bytes of a file, never the whole thing. A phone
      photograph is tens of megabytes and the format check needs thirty-two of
      them; reading it whole would stall the tab on exactly the devices least
      able to afford it, and would do so before we know it is an image at all.

SEO  n/a — a client component behind no route until #28.

TESTS
  38 unit + axe on the component, 19 on the upload utilities, 23 on the
  failure resolver, 6 on the FileList stub. 100% coverage, no suppressions.

  12 Playwright tests in a NEW `component-behaviour` project, run against the
  built Storybook. Real picker, real DataTransfer, real ClipboardEvent, real
  browser. jsdom can construct none of those, so the unit suite reaches them
  through a stub — this suite is what proves the stub is not lying. Mounted
  through Storybook rather than a route because a component is reachable there
  the day it is written rather than the day something mounts it.

  6 stories: idle, dragging, busy, two refusals, mobile. The dragging state is
  driven by a real dragenter dispatched from a callback ref, which React runs
  during commit — so the highlighted frame is the first one painted and the
  screenshot cannot catch an un-highlighted one. No hover story: hover carries
  no visual state on the zone, only the invisible warm-up, and a screenshot
  identical to idle is a baseline that asserts nothing.

  FLAKE FOUND — and MISDIAGNOSED here; see #28a for the real cause. The note
  that stood in this space blamed the host's font stack for the Design
  system/Tokens baselines differing, and concluded the fix was a bundled mono
  face. It was not the host. Baselines were being written by a Chromium build
  that Playwright does not pin, and the mono glyphs are simply where two
  Chromium builds diverge first. Everything reverted as "drift" over three PRs
  was correct all along.

  NEW GUARD, src/theme/token-references.test.ts: every var(--tk-…) anywhere in
  the codebase must name a token the stylesheet declares, and must carry no
  fallback. The existing parity test checked only that registered tokens are
  declared — the direction that never fails. A component naming a token that
  does not exist passes build, lint and stylelint, then silently falls back.
  Five such references were already in the tree; all are fixed. The fallback
  ban is part of the same rule: once existence is proven, a fallback is dead
  code whose only effect is to make the next typo look plausible.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #27 — Live camera capture with real-time guidance
Branch: feat/camera-capture            Depends on: #26               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  PULLED FORWARD from v1.1 into v1, on competitive evidence. PhotoAiD's mobile
  apps have this and we would be the only serious tool without it.

  It removes the retake loop entirely, which is the single loudest complaint in
  every competitor's reviews: people upload, get rejected, upload again, and give
  up around the fourth attempt. Live feedback replaces that with "move closer…
  closer… hold there".

  Cheap for what it delivers: getUserMedia plus the geometry engine from #17,
  both of which already exist by this point.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] getUserMedia capture with rear/front camera switching
  [x] Live overlay: a fixed head oval, a real-time head-height readout, and a
      background-uniformity warning before the shutter rather than after
  [x] Analysis on a throttled frame loop, not per frame
  [x] Capture at the sensor's full resolution, not the preview's
  [x] Falls back cleanly to file upload wherever camera access is unavailable —
      and alongside it where it is. Most desktops have no camera worth using
      and plenty of readers already have a better photograph on their phone; a
      camera-only screen turns those people away at the first step.

  ONE INSTRUCTION AT A TIME. The guidance engine produces an ordered list and
  the overlay renders exactly the first item. A live view that lists five
  faults gets none of them fixed — the reader reads, looks up, and the picture
  has already changed. The loop the plan asked for, "move closer… closer…
  hold there", only works if there is one thing to do.

  EVERY THRESHOLD IS IMPORTED, NONE ARE CHOSEN. Level, confident, plain, far
  enough away — all come from the constants the still analyser uses. Guidance
  with its own numbers would walk somebody into a photograph the analyser then
  rejects, having told them it was fine, which is worse than no guidance at
  all because they followed it.

  [~] REFACTOR: planRawCrop extracted from planCrop into crop-geometry.utils.
      Where the crop goes is now computed in ONE place and used by both the
      still pipeline and the live preview. Two derivations would have let the
      preview say "hold there" for a frame the analyser then refused. What is
      left in planCrop is the part the camera does not want: refusing.

  [~] FIXED, found by writing the guidance tests: the landmark point order —
      chin, left eye, right eye — was a comment inside the fake detector,
      which is to say a convention two consumers could disagree about while
      both compiled. It is now landmark-points.constants.ts, and a test pins
      the fake to it.

  [~] FIXED, found by chasing an uncovered branch: unmounting while
      getUserMedia was still in flight leaked the camera. The cleanup ran
      before the stream existed, found nothing to stop, and the indicator
      light stayed on over an empty page — on the one product where a light
      that stays on is the accusation. There is now a mounted flag, and the
      stream is stopped on arrival if the reader has already gone.

  [~] FIXED, same way: the background was judged by indexing a segmentation
      mask with frame-pixel indices. For a mask at a different resolution
      index n names a different pixel in each, so the verdict would have been
      computed from a scrambled selection and delivered with full confidence.
      A size mismatch now reports "not judged", which is always available and
      always honest.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Permission denied — named as the browser blocking it, with where the
      button is. Eight distinct failure codes, each with its own remedy,
      because "camera unavailable" collapses them all into an apology.
  [x] No camera present (most desktops) — told to upload instead, not sent
      hunting through settings for a device they do not have.
  [x] iOS Safari refuses getUserMedia over http, and does it by withholding
      the whole API — which looks exactly like having no camera. Checked
      separately and blamed on the protocol, because telling somebody to buy a
      webcam when they need https would be wrong.
  [x] Preview resolution far below capture resolution — guidance is computed
      on videoWidth/videoHeight, the STREAM's size, never the element's. The
      browser suite asserts videoWidth exceeds clientWidth, which is the whole
      distinction in one line.
  [~] Device rotation mid-session — the frame grab reads the stream's current
      dimensions every tick, so a rotated frame is measured as it arrives
      rather than against a size cached at start. NOT verified on a physical
      device; no phone in CI. Flagged for #34 with the iOS capture checks.
  [x] Front camera mirroring: the preview is flipped by a CSS transform on the
      element and the capture is not. drawImage reads the stream, which was
      never flipped, so captureStill has no un-mirroring step and must never
      grow one — a mirrored passport photograph is rejected for the parting
      being on the wrong side, and the reader cannot see it coming. Asserted
      in the browser (the transform is on the element) and in the unit suite
      (drawImage gets a plain destination rectangle and nothing else).
  [x] Battery and thermal cost — the interval is measured from the END of the
      last analysis, not on a fixed schedule. setInterval at 300ms where
      detection takes 400ms does not give three analyses a second; it gives an
      unbounded queue, a hot phone, and guidance about where somebody's head
      was several seconds ago. A hidden tab stops the loop entirely.
  [x] Stream stopped on unmount — and on stop, on failure, on camera switch,
      and on arriving after the reader has already left.
  [x] Low light, where guidance would be confidently wrong — reported BEFORE
      anything read off the detector. Landmarks from a dark room come back
      with points and a confidence, both wrong, and "move left" on that basis
      is worse than admitting the light is the problem. The threshold is far
      below any exposure judgement, deliberately: a band on face luminance is
      a band on skin tone (see src/quality/exposure.utils.ts).

SEO
  [x] Camera UI is client-only and enters no static HTML: nothing imports it
      from a route yet, and the build output is unchanged.

TESTS
  158 unit + axe across the camera layer and its two components. 100%
  coverage, no suppressions.

  8 Playwright tests against Chromium's fake capture device
  (--use-fake-device-for-media-stream), which gives getUserMedia a real
  MediaStream. Real tracks, real frames, real readyState — the things jsdom
  cannot produce at all. They drive the SAME story the screenshots use, with
  its stub environment unset through Storybook's `!undefined` URL arg: a
  dedicated real-camera story would have been captured by the screenshot suite
  too, and its baseline would then depend on whether the runner had a camera.

  10 stories, 30 baselines: the oval at each state, the ready ring, and the
  two refusals that matter most. The refusal stories press their own button
  from a callback ref, which React runs during commit, so the refused state is
  the first frame painted and the screenshot cannot race it.

PR #28 — Result panel
Branch: feat/result-panel              Depends on: #27               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  What the checks concluded, and what they are still doing. The answer at the
  top in one sentence, then every rule with what was measured and the physical
  correction, then the list of things only the reader can judge.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Overall verdict, per-rule rows with measurement and fix instruction
  [x] Manual checklist as its own list; the annotated overlay and downloads are
      passed in by the page rather than owned here
  [x] Progress and error states

  THE ROWS AND THE PDF SHARE ONE FORMATTER. resolveRuleMessage already produced
  exactly the four strings a row shows, so the panel calls it rather than
  formatting again. Two formatters is how a downloadable report and the screen
  it was generated from end up disagreeing about the same photograph.

  [~] CHANGED: row verdicts are now short ("Meets it", "Does not meet it") and
      the headline keeps the sentence. They were the same string: every one of
      twenty-four rows repeated "Meets the published requirements" down a
      column, burying the two rows that did not, which are the only reason
      anybody is reading it. The PDF report picks this up too.

  [~] ADDED to RuleDefinition: `measures`, whether a rule reports a quantity.
      Needed BEFORE the rule runs — see the CLS note below — and verified by a
      test that evaluates every rule against a measured photograph and asserts
      the flag matches. A declaration that could drift would be worse than none.

  [~] FIXED: percentages were formatted to two decimal places, so a rough
      physical instruction read "your head needs to be about 111.67% taller".
      Nobody can act on the .67, and the precision makes a rule of thumb read
      as a measurement.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Zero CLS — and it is MEASURED, not asserted in a comment. The waiting
      state is exactly as tall as the answer: 0px on a passing report, checked
      in a real browser on every run.

      Three versions of this passed their unit tests while jumping. The first
      reserved the rule rows and forgot the verdict block, both headings and
      the entire manual checklist: 332px, a third of the panel. The second
      reserved a measurement line on every row when only eight of twenty-four
      rules report one: 225px the other way. The third computed the verdict
      block's height from its padding and line box and was two pixels out,
      because the block is sized by its icon.

      What works is refusing to compute any of it. The row count comes from
      asking the real engine what this specification will produce, with the
      evidence bundle that says nothing ran. The row and verdict placeholders
      import the real components' own stylesheets, so their heights are not
      matched — they are the same boxes. The progress bar's space is held in
      every state, including the finished one, or it would move everything
      below it twice.

      RESIDUAL, and it cannot be reserved: a failing row carries a line of
      advice a passing row does not (+47px on the mixed fixture), and a photo
      where a stage produced no number leaves reserved lines unused (-179px).
      Both depend on the verdict, which is what the reader is waiting for.
      Bounded by the browser test at a quarter of the viewport so they cannot
      quietly become a third of the panel again.

  [x] aria-live announcement when analysis completes — ONE region, carrying the
      whole sentence. The first version had a live region on the verdict block
      as well, which said the verdict twice and never said the wait was over.
  [x] Pass/fail never relies on colour alone: icon shape, text label and colour,
      all three, in the rows and in the headline. Under forced-colors every
      status token resolves to CanvasText, and roughly one in twelve men cannot
      separate the pass green from the fail red.
  [x] Long rule lists on small screens — rows wrap rather than truncate, and the
      mobile screenshots cover the longest row and the longest verdict.
  [x] Analysis cancelled or superseded — NOT an error. Cancelling is the state
      the reader asked for by choosing another photograph, and an error box
      explaining that they did what they meant to do is noise sitting exactly
      where the next answer should go. The panel returns to waiting.
  [x] Slow devices: progress moves because it is driven by the STAGE first and
      the ratio second. ANALYSIS_STAGES is ordered so a bar can be moved by the
      stage alone, which on a slow device is the only thing that moves — the
      segmenter reports no intermediate ratio worth the name, and a bar reading
      the ratio only sits at zero for several seconds and reads as a hang.

SEO  n/a — nothing imports it from a route yet; the build output is unchanged.

TESTS
  116 unit + axe across the panel and its four components, 14 on the state
  utilities. 100% coverage, no suppressions.

  7 Playwright tests in the component-behaviour project, and they are the only
  reason this entry can claim zero CLS: layout is a property of rendered
  pixels, and jsdom has none. They measure the panel in each state and compare.

  15 stories: waiting, analysing, all-pass, mixed, nothing-measurable, two
  failures, and each placeholder next to the real component it stands in for —
  which is the comparison that would show a height mismatch at a glance.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #28a — Screenshot baselines were written by the wrong browser
Branch: fix/visual-baseline-browser    Depends on: #28               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  A repair, and a correction to three earlier entries.

  #28 merged with the `visual` job red, so main was red. Six baselines failed:
  Result/ResultPanel · All Pass and · Mixed, in all three variants.

ROOT CAUSE
  Baselines were being generated with Chromium 1194. Playwright 1.62.1 pins
  1234, and that is what CI installs. Two Chromium builds rasterise the same
  page almost identically — the diff image is pixel-perfect across every
  label, icon, heading and border, and differs ONLY on the monospace
  measurement lines, which is where they diverge first.

  That "only the mono text" signature is exactly what was seen on the Design
  system/Tokens stories in #26, #27 and #28, and it was diagnosed as the host
  machine's font stack resolving `ui-monospace` differently. It was not. The
  same six failures reproduce locally the moment the override is removed, and
  the token baselines pass — they were never drifting. They were reverted as
  noise three times while they were the only correct files in the diff.

  The lesson is narrower than "check the browser version": a screenshot diff
  reads as "somebody changed the design", and that reading is convincing
  enough to survive three rounds. Nothing about the failure pointed at the
  tool, so the tool was never suspected.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] The six ResultPanel baselines regenerated with the pinned Chromium. No
      other file changed, which is itself the evidence: every other baseline
      already matched, including ones written under 1194.
  [x] A guard in playwright.config.ts. PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE is still
      honoured for RUNNING the suite — it is genuinely needed in a sandbox —
      but WRITING baselines with it now throws, naming the reason. The
      escape hatch was the defect; removing it entirely would break running
      the suite here at all.
  [x] The misdiagnosis in the #26 entry corrected rather than deleted, so the
      wrong conclusion and its correction are both on the record.

TESTS
  The full stories suite green against the pinned browser: 286 passed, 0
  failed. Guard verified by running an update with the override set and
  watching it refuse.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #28b — The checker page: decode in the browser, analyse, and answer
Branch: feat/browser-decoder           Depends on: #28a              Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Twenty-eight PRs of engine, and no URL that ran it. Every piece had tests
  and none of them were wired to each other in production code: nothing
  decoded a photograph in a browser, nothing assembled what the rule engine
  reads from a decode, and no page mounted the upload zone next to the result
  panel. This is the PR where the product becomes a thing a person can use.

  Split out of #29 deliberately. Country pages are the ranking surface, and
  building forty of them on top of an engine nobody had run end to end would
  have been forty pages of the same broken checker.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Browser decoder: createImageBitmap with the browser's EXIF handling OFF
      (the orientation is applied here, from the file's own EXIF, so a photo
      is never rotated twice), downscaled to the analysis edge, read back as
      pixels, bitmap always closed. sRGB, because the exported file carries no
      colour profile and a P3 photo labelled sRGB prints with the skin tones
      pushed up.
  [x] Animated-source detection by walking the container — GIF blocks, PNG
      chunks up to IDAT, RIFF/WebP chunks — rather than searching for "ANIM"
      or "acTL", which occur inside compressed pixel data constantly.
  [x] buildRuleInput: the missing half of the pipeline. Landmarks in normalised
      space become source pixels, the crown is estimated on the mask in
      WORKING space, the crop is planned, and the eye separation is reported in
      the EXPORTED file's pixels — which is what a border system actually
      measures.
  [x] /passport-photo-checker, server-rendered, with the specification list
      resolved on the server and passed down.
  [x] CheckerPanel: pick a document, add a photo, read the answer. The decoder
      and the worker are built on FIRST USE, not on render — the models are
      fifteen megabytes and nothing should load them because a page rendered.
  [x] Ingestion failures appear on the dropzone, analysis failures in the
      result panel. Two kinds of failure, two places, which is the whole
      reason they are separate types.
  [x] The analysis worker is now built by scripts/build-worker.mjs into
      public/workers, not by the application bundler. See ROOT CAUSE below.
  [x] The sitemap no longer advertises /rejected, which 404s. Search Console
      reports advertised 404s as errors against the whole property.

ROOT CAUSE — the worker was served as TypeScript
  The page shipped in a state where every unit test passed and the checker
  told the reader "The checks stopped unexpectedly." The worker was being
  emitted as /_next/static/media/analysis.worker.<hash>.ts — the raw
  TypeScript source, copied out as a static asset. The browser cannot parse
  it, so the worker died before running a line.

  Turbopack does not compile `new Worker(new URL('./x.ts', import.meta.url))`
  as a worker entry point; it resolves the URL as an asset reference. Proved
  by experiment rather than assumed: a .js probe worker was copied out the
  same way. The build succeeds either way, which is what makes it dangerous —
  nothing fails until a real browser tries to run it.

  Second failure behind the first: with the worker finally loading, MediaPipe
  died with "ModuleFactory not set". Its WASM runtime is a classic script that
  declares `var ModuleFactory` and expects to read it back off the global; in
  a module worker that declaration is module-scoped and the global stays
  empty. So the worker is classic, and the bundle is an IIFE with nothing left
  to import at runtime.

EDGE CASES
  [x] A file that passes the header check and dies in the decoder — a transfer
      that stopped halfway — goes back to the dropzone, not to the answer.
  [x] A browser with no Worker at all (locked-down enterprise, some private
      modes) is told the checks cannot run here, not shown a TypeError.
  [x] An empty specification registry renders nothing rather than a dropzone
      that leads nowhere.
  [x] A GIF with global and local colour tables — which is almost every real
      GIF — is walked correctly rather than read as blocks.
  [x] A truncated GIF whose sub-blocks run off the end terminates the walk.
  [x] An analysis error with no code reports as unknown rather than borrowing
      a remedy for a failure that did not happen.

TESTS
  2726 unit tests, 100% coverage held. Playwright: four specs against the real
  page — including one that uploads a real JPEG and drives the actual worker,
  the actual models and the actual detector to a verdict, and one that asserts
  the worker is served as JavaScript. Four component-behaviour specs driving
  the panel through a real picker in Chromium. Three stories with baselines.

  The engine spec is the one that matters: it is the only test in the suite
  that would have caught this bug, and every other kind of test passed while
  the page was broken.

NOTES
  - analyse-photo.test.ts builds its photograph once, in beforeAll. Thirteen
    tests each regenerating a 1200x1200 head and measuring every pixel of it
    passed locally and timed out the moment coverage instrumentation was on.
  - browser-decode-environment.ts is excluded from coverage: it is the
    createImageBitmap/OffscreenCanvas seam and nothing else, and it is
    exercised for real by the Chromium spec above.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #28c — "Take a photo" opens a camera
Branch: fix/camera-button              Depends on: #28b             Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Reported from the live site: the button did nothing.

ROOT CAUSE
  "Take a photo" was `<input type="file" capture="user">`. The `capture`
  attribute is ignored by every desktop browser — Chrome, Firefox and Safari
  on a laptop all open the ordinary file picker — so the button was a second
  "Choose a photo" wearing a camera's label. It only ever opened a camera on
  a phone.

  Behind it, the same gap as the checker itself: CameraCapture had been built
  in #26, with a live stream, a guidance overlay and 34 tests, and NOTHING
  MOUNTED IT. The product's whole differentiator — "move closer, hold there"
  before the shutter instead of a rejection after it — existed only in
  Storybook.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] "Take a photo" opens the live guided camera on the checker page.
  [x] The photograph it takes goes through the same ingestion and analysis an
      uploaded one does, and lands in the same report.
  [x] The preview runs on the panel's own analysis client, so the models load
      once and the live guidance costs no second worker.
  [x] Preview progress is deliberately dropped rather than shown: a bar moving
      under a camera nobody has photographed with yet is progress towards a
      report that does not exist.
  [x] CameraCapture reports upward when the camera cannot open at all.

EDGE CASES
  [x] A browser with no getUserMedia — an in-app webview, a page on plain http
      — fails every time it is asked. After the first refusal the dropzone
      stops offering the camera and goes back to the phone's own camera app,
      which needs no getUserMedia and shoots at full sensor resolution.
      Offering the same dead button twice is how a reader decides a product is
      broken.
  [x] "Upload a photo instead" returns to the dropzone from the camera.
  [x] The capture input is still what UploadZone renders when its caller has
      no camera to offer, so the phone path is not dead code.

TESTS
  21 unit tests on the panel including the whole camera route, 2 on the zone,
  1 on the component, all at 100% coverage; 2 component-behaviour specs and
  1 real-page spec in Chromium. The canvas stub moved to src/testing so both
  suites drive a real capture through it, and it now emits real JPEG bytes —
  a blob of zeros is refused by ingestion, which would have made every test of
  the capture route assert a refusal it did not mean.

PR #29 — Country page template and static generation
Branch: feat/country-pages             Depends on: #28c              Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
IMPLEMENTS
  [x] /[country]/[document] route, generateStaticParams over the SERVABLE
      registry. Four pages today; the same code serves forty.
  [x] Checker above the fold, requirements table and FAQ below. The checker is
      first because somebody searching "us passport photo size" has a photo in
      their hand — the table is what they read when the answer surprises them.
  [x] Per-page metadata, and FAQ/HowTo/Breadcrumb JSON-LD built from the same
      arrays the page renders, so the structured data cannot promise text the
      reader cannot see.
  [x] "Requirements last verified on <date>" with the authority's own link,
      part of the table component rather than a decoration beside it.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Unknown country or document 404s with a real status. `dynamicParams =
      false` means the router answers before the page runs — a soft 404 gets
      indexed, competes with the real pages, and never goes away.
  [x] Every requirement is in the server HTML. Asserted twice: once against the
      built files in CI, once by crawling the running site.
  [x] Near-duplicate content. THE FAQ ANSWERS ARE BUILT FROM THE
      SPECIFICATION, so two countries never answer "what size is a passport
      photo?" the same way. A test asserts the size and head-height answers are
      distinct across every served spec — and deliberately does NOT demand that
      every answer differ, because some requirements genuinely are identical
      and inventing a difference would be worse than reporting the sameness.
  [x] Several accepted sizes: alternativePrintSizes appears under the print
      size, so nobody reprints a photo that was already acceptable.
  [x] Long country names: metadata length is advisory rather than fatal, as
      before. An over-long title is a writing problem, not a build failure.
  [~] ISR revalidation is NOT implemented. Every page is statically generated
      at build time and a spec change ships with a deploy, which is how the
      registry already works — there is no runtime source to revalidate
      against. Worth revisiting only if specs ever move out of the repository.

FOUND WHILE CRAWLING — three broken links, on every page of the site
  The e2e crawl asserts no link on a country page 404s. It failed three times,
  each on something that had been shipping for weeks:
  [x] The header and footer both linked /why-was-my-passport-photo-rejected,
      which has no page. The sitemap had already dropped it for exactly that
      reason and the navigation kept pointing at it.
  [x] The footer linked Privacy and Terms, neither of which exists. The reader
      who clicks Privacy on a page promising their photo never leaves their
      device is the last reader who should meet a 404. The column is hidden
      until the pages land, and the links are a prop so the column that will
      hold them still renders and is still tested.
  [x] The footer hard-coded every country link to `passport`, so Schengen —
      which publishes a visa standard and no passport one — pointed at a page
      that does not exist. Footer links now come from the registry.

SEO
  [x] scripts/assert-seo.ts DISCOVERS the generated pages from the build output
      rather than listing them, so a country added tomorrow is asserted without
      anyone remembering to add it. Per route: title, description, canonical,
      H1, JSON-LD, the requirements table, the verification date, the source
      link, and no unfilled copy placeholder.

TESTS
  36 unit tests across the four new builders at 100% coverage, plus 9 Playwright
  specs that crawl every route in the sitemap: status, canonical, single H1,
  requirements in the served HTML, real 404s for unknown countries and
  documents, no broken link, and one that drives the real engine against a
  country's own specification.

NOTES
  - The spec picker is hidden when there is one specification. A question with
    one answer reads as though the page were unsure which country it is about.
  - File sizes are decimal, not binary: the DS-160 publishes "240 KB" meaning
    240,000 bytes, and 1024 would print 234 beside an official 240.
  - Durations are spelt out. Intl's short form gives "6 mths", which reads as a
    typo in a sentence about how recent a photograph must be.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #30a — Dimension route family
Branch: feat/alt-route-families        Depends on: #29              Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SPLIT FROM #30, and the reason is in the plan's own edge cases: "reverse-mode
needs its own entry flow, not a redirect". That is a feature, not a content
page, and bundling it with four size pages makes one PR nobody can review.
The problem-page family is #30b.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] /2x2-inch-photo, /35x45mm-photo, /600x600-photo, /resize-photo-to-240kb.
      /50x70mm-photo is declared and deliberately does not render — see below.
  [x] A catalogue of size families with three kinds, because the numbers people
      are handed are three different things: a printed size, a pixel size for
      an upload, and a file-size ceiling. A union rather than one shape with
      optional fields, so a family cannot half-declare itself.
  [x] Cross-linked both ways. The country page owns the requirements and points
      at the number; the size page owns "who else asks for this" and points
      back at the countries.

EDGE CASES
  [x] One size serving many countries: that IS the page. Somebody searching
      "2x2 photo" has been handed a number by a form and does not know whose
      rule it is — the list of countries is the answer, and it is also what
      keeps the page off the thin-content pile.
  [x] Overlap with country pages is one-directional by design. The size page
      states its number, names who asks for it, and leaves the full
      requirements to the page that owns them. No requirement table is
      duplicated; each page canonicalises to itself.
  [x] A size no VERIFIED specification uses gets no page. 50x70mm is real and
      several countries use it, but none is verified, and a page about a
      requirement nobody checked is the one thing this must not publish. A test
      asserts it is in the catalogue and absent from the routes.
  [x] Slugs are the search phrase, not the stored unit. The registry holds
      50.8mm; nobody has ever searched for that. The matching is done on the
      measurement, so the two can differ without either being wrong.
  [x] Print sizes match within a tenth of a millimetre — 50.8 and 50.75 are the
      same square — but 50mm and 51mm stay separate pages.
  [x] A pixel size matches a RANGE (600 satisfies a 600-1200 form); a file size
      matches EXACTLY (a 10MB limit does not answer "resize to 240KB").

ROUTING
  [x] The country segment is now [slug]/[document] rather than
      [country]/[document]. Next allows one dynamic segment name per depth, and
      the size pages are single-segment routes that share the root with it. The
      URLs are unchanged.

TESTS
  18 unit tests on the catalogue and the labels at 100% coverage, plus 7
  Playwright specs: every size page renders, names countries, links only to
  pages that exist, is linked back from them, and an unpublished size 404s.
  SEO assertions now cover 10 routes, discovering the size pages from the build
  output the same way they discover the country ones.

NOTES
  - The unit goes on the second number only: "2 × 2 in", not "2 in × 2 in".
  - The checker on a size page carries every specification that uses the size,
    so the picker is a real choice there — unlike a country page, where it is
    hidden because there is only one answer.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #30b — Problem route family
Branch: feat/problem-pages             Depends on: #30a             Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The mirror of a country page. A country page answers "what does the United
States want?"; these answer "what does everybody want about head size?" and
"why did mine come back?" — same registry, different axis.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] /why-was-my-passport-photo-rejected — every reason a photo comes back,
      built from ALL_RULES rather than written as a list. The page cannot
      describe a rule the engine does not measure, and cannot fall silent about
      one it does. The order is the registry's own, which is the order worth
      dealing with them in.
  [x] /passport-photo-head-size and /passport-photo-background-check — one
      requirement compared across every served specification, rendered by the
      same RequirementsTable a country page uses, with the rows being countries
      instead of requirements.
  [x] The header and footer link to the rejected page again — PR #29 removed
      the link because it pointed at a 404 on every page of the site.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Reverse mode is its own entry flow, not a redirect. Somebody arriving
      with a rejection letter already has the verdict and wants the diagnosis,
      so the reasons come BEFORE the upload. A Playwright test asserts the
      ordering by bounding box rather than by DOM order, because the thing that
      matters is what is above the fold, not what is earlier in the markup.
  [x] Each page says something the country pages do not. The rejection page
      carries the 24 rules; the head-size page carries both units AND the crown
      definition, which is the reason the same photograph passes in one country
      and fails in another; the background page carries the fact that the answer
      is not the same everywhere, which is the assumption it exists to correct.
  [x] Every reason is in the SERVED HTML. The reader who needs it most arrived
      from a search, and a crawler that must run JavaScript to find the reasons
      may never see them.
  [x] Rows are matched by role=rowheader in the tests, not by text: the checker
      below the table offers the same countries as radio labels, and matching
      either would pass while asserting nothing about the comparison.
  [x] FAQ structured data on the rejection page, canonical at its own URL on all
      three, and no link on any of them leads anywhere that does not exist.

TESTS
  11 unit tests on the reasons and the comparison rows at 100% coverage —
  including that no placeholder is left unfilled and that the background values
  are not all identical — plus 7 Playwright specs. Coverage stands at
  3820/3820 statements, 1520 branches, 761 functions. SEO assertions now cover
  13 routes, discovered from the build output. 18 header and footer visual
  baselines regenerated because the navigation link came back.


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 PHASE 6 — SCALE AND LAUNCH
