───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #31a — Registry expansion, first European tranche
Branch: feat/registry-europe            Depends on: #30b            Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SPLIT FROM #31, on evidence gathered while starting it. The target is still
100+; what changed is that the grind turned out to have a shape, and the shape
needs code before it needs countries.

DESCRIPTION
  Three countries, and the three schema changes they forced. France, Germany
  and the Netherlands are neighbours who publish the same photo size and
  disagree about almost everything else, which makes them the best possible
  first tranche: they break every assumption the registry had absorbed from
  four English-speaking specs.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] France, Germany and the Netherlands, verified against the issuing
      authority's own published guidance, with source and date recorded.
  [x] `digital` is now OPTIONAL. A digital requirement is something an
      authority publishes when you can upload the photo yourself. Where
      submission goes through a booth, an authorised photographer or the
      counter, there is no upload to constrain and the authority states a
      printed size and nothing about pixels. Same treatment the eye line
      already had: no row rather than an invented one.
  [x] `print.dpi` is now optional, for the same reason. Most authorities never
      state one. `exportDpi()` supplies our own rendering resolution where they
      did not — kept deliberately separate from the published field so it can
      never appear in a requirements table as though a government had said it.
  [x] `maxAgeMonths` is now optional. "Six months" is repeated so widely it
      reads like a universal rule; Germany's published standard states no
      figure at all. The photo-age check has a second message for that case
      rather than quoting a rule the reader's government never wrote.
  [x] A `submission` field — self-service, authorised-photographer,
      authority-capture — with its own requirements row.
  [x] `npm run audit:sources`: fetches every cited source and reports dead
      links, redirects and specs past the re-verification window.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Requirements published only as a PDF. Germany's numbers live on a
      printable template, not a web page, so the template is what is cited and
      what was read.
  [x] Countries that reject a self-taken photo entirely. France requires an
      authorised photographer or booth; Germany since 1 May 2025 requires
      capture at the authority itself. Both still publish the requirements, so
      checking a photo is still worth doing — you find out what is wrong before
      you pay for the session rather than after.
  [x] Non-white backgrounds, and one authority that FORBIDS white: "Le fond
      blanc est interdit". The exact opposite of the United States rule, and
      the sharpest evidence in the registry that one photograph cannot serve
      two applications.
  [x] The same size measured differently. France measures to the top of the
      skull with hair excluded, Germany to the top of the head as photographed.
      Same 35 x 45mm photo, same 70-80% proportion, four millimetres of
      tolerance, and the difference eats most of it.
  [x] Child exemptions recorded in notes where published (Germany 50-80% under
      10, Netherlands 19-30mm under 11).
  [x] An authority publishing a measurement no other does — the Dutch face
      width, 16 to 20mm ear to ear — goes in notes, not the schema. One
      authority's extra number does not earn a field every other spec leaves
      empty.

BLOCKED, AND WHY  (recorded so the next tranche is planned, not rediscovered)
  Most authority sites refuse automated requests from a datacentre address.
  Verified unreachable from CI and dev egress: the United States
  (travel.state.gov 403), Canada (canada.ca refused outright), Australia,
  Japan, New Zealand's passport office, South Korea, Turkey, Pakistan, Nigeria
  and South Africa. Reachable and readable: France, Germany, the Netherlands,
  Ireland, Italy, Spain, Singapore, Brazil, China, the UAE and the EU's own
  document server.
  This is a sourcing constraint, not a code one. The next tranches take the
  reachable list; the blocked ones need their text supplied or the egress
  policy widened.

  Ireland is deliberately NOT in this tranche. Its guidance publishes a print
  size range, a pixel minimum, background, glasses and head covering — and no
  head height at all. A spec cannot be completed without inventing the one
  number the whole geometry hangs on.

TESTS
  26 new unit tests at 100% coverage (3150 total), plus 8 Playwright specs
  asserting each page says the thing that is true of ITS country and would be
  wrong of its neighbour — a tranche of three near-identical pages would pass a
  page-exists test and be worthless. Including that Germany and France
  disagree about the crown, that Germany's two published forms of the head band
  agree with each other, that a country publishing no pixel requirement matches
  a print size family and no pixel family, and that no FAQ asks a question the
  authority did not answer. The source audit found two stale citations on its
  first run: service-public.fr had moved to service-public.gouv.fr and the
  German PDF had moved host. Both fixed here.

  One existing test failed for the best possible reason: the soft-404 check
  used /france/passport-photo as its example of an unknown country, and France
  became real. Repointed at a slug that will never be a country rather than
  weakened.

  A LATENT BUG FOUND BY THE FAILING BASELINES. CountryLinkGrid defaulted to
  COUNTRY_SLUGS while its own doc comment promised "every country served".
  Those sets differ by seven — canada, australia, india, japan, china, brazil
  and new-zealand have slugs reserved and no verified spec — so the default
  rendered seven links to 404s. The single real caller passes an explicit list,
  so nothing shipped broken, but adding slugs ahead of specs is exactly what
  the remaining tranches do, and the next caller to omit the prop would have
  shipped them. Now defaults to listServedCountries(), with a test that fails
  if any unserved slug is ever linked.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #31c — HEIC photographs, decoded rather than refused
Branch: feat/heic-decode                Depends on: #31a            Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
UNPLANNED, and it should not have been. HEIC is how every iPhone saves a photo
by default, and this product's whole audience is people photographing
themselves with a phone.

DESCRIPTION
  The registry work turned this up sideways: Singapore's ICA lists heic and
  heif among the file types it accepts, and we did not accept them at all.

  What was there before recognised HEIC precisely so it could refuse it, with
  instructions to go and convert the file by hand. That refusal was decided by
  a fixed list of natively decodable formats — which meant it fired on EVERY
  browser, including Safari and iOS, which open HEIC natively and are exactly
  where iPhone photographs are. Those readers were told their browser could not
  open their own camera roll.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Decodability is asked of the decoder rather than looked up in a list, so
      Safari and iOS decode HEIC natively at a cost of nothing.
  [x] A libheif WebAssembly fallback for browsers that genuinely cannot — the
      Windows or Android reader who was SENT an iPhone photo.
  [x] Native first, always. The fallback runs only after the browser has
      refused, so the megabyte and a half never reaches the people most likely
      to be holding a HEIC.
  [x] NO TRANSCODE. The obvious build converts HEIC to JPEG and decodes that,
      spending a whole lossy generation before the photo is even measured. This
      decodes straight to RGBA and feeds the existing pipeline, so the only
      lossy step is the export the reader submits — one generation, not two.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Orientation. A HEIC keeps its rotation in container boxes this pipeline
      does not parse, not the JPEG EXIF block it does. HEIC alone asks the
      browser to apply the file's own orientation; every other format still has
      it applied once, by us. Getting this wrong lands portrait photos on their
      side, or rotates them twice.
  [x] A container holding several images — a thumbnail, the frames of a Live
      Photo, the exposures of a burst. Taking the first would eventually hand
      somebody their own thumbnail, upscaled, and every sharpness check would
      fail on a photograph that was never blurry. Largest wins, ties to the
      earliest, which is the primary item.
  [x] Freeing every frame, not only the chosen one. libheif allocates on the
      WebAssembly heap where the JavaScript collector cannot see it, and a
      burst decoded and dropped leaks the lot — the SECOND upload of a session
      is the one that runs out of memory.
  [x] A corrupt file reporting a nonsense size: refused before anything is
      allocated for it, rather than throwing on a negative array length.
  [x] Both routes failing: still a refusal the reader can act on, not a crash.

LICENSING
  libheif is LGPL-3.0 and this was put to the architect before it was added.
  Shipped as its own lazily loaded chunk, unmodified — the dynamic-linking case
  the licence is friendliest to. Our own source is unaffected.

TESTS
  13 unit tests on the container decisions at 100% coverage, plus 4 Playwright
  specs in a real browser. Two of those are the ones that matter: the decoder
  chunk is ABSENT from the initial payload, and it IS fetched when a HEIC
  arrives. That pair is what retires the bundler risk this project has already
  been bitten by once — a feature that passed every unit test and was dead in
  the browser because a module was not built the way the code assumed.

NOT COVERED, and worth knowing
  No automated test decodes a REAL HEIC. Every sample file findable from this
  environment is a third-party photograph under unclear licensing, and one of
  those does not belong in this repository for the sake of a fixture. The unit
  tests use a fake container; the browser tests cover loading. A genuine iPhone
  photograph against the deployed site is the missing confirmation.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #31b.. — Registry expansion, remaining tranches
Branch: feat/registry-<tranche>        Depends on: #31a             Status: TODO
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  The grind, and the moat. Every spec verified against the issuing authority's
  own page, with the URL and the date recorded. No spec enters without a source.

  TARGET RAISED from 40 to 100+, on competitive evidence. PhotoAiD and Visafoto
  both cover 200+ document types. Every country we do not cover is a bounce from
  a visitor who already found us — and the long tail is the ONLY ground a new
  domain can win, because the head terms belong to incumbents for years.
  Under-covering the tail forfeits the one advantage available to us.

  This is also the single biggest lever on revenue. Ad income is a function of
  traffic, traffic is a function of pages that can rank, and each verified
  country is one such page.
EDGE CASES
  [ ] Authority pages that state requirements only in a PDF
  [ ] Rules differing for applications filed abroad
  [ ] Child and infant exemptions
  [ ] Countries permitting or requiring glasses
  [ ] Non-white background requirements (light grey, cream, light blue)
  [ ] Countries that mandate a physical print and reject digital entirely
TESTS  Every spec parses; a snapshot test catches accidental edits.

